This is the kind of material I wanted to be able to write for a fullscreen effect:

```ts
const material = defineMaterial({
  fragment: `
    fn frag(uv: vec2f) -> vec4f {
      return vec4f(uv, sin(time) * 0.5 + 0.5, 1.0);
    }
  `,
  uniforms: {
    time: 0.0,
    resolution: [0, 0],
  },
  textures: {
    noise: { url: '/textures/noise.png' },
  },
});
```

The shader gets time and resolution as uniforms, and the material can provide textures. For a noise field, a generative background, or a post-processing effect, that is often most of what I need to describe.

I had been reaching for Three.js to do this. It brought roughly 600 kB of dependency along with concepts intended for 3D scenes: geometry, materials, cameras, lights, and loaders. Setting up a fragment effect meant working through things like `PlaneGeometry`, `ShaderMaterial`, and `OrthographicCamera` just to draw a quad. Three.js is useful when I need a scene graph. Here I wanted to run a WGSL function for each pixel and, sometimes, chain a few passes afterward.

[Spektral](https://github.com/kaltwrk/spektral) grew out of that narrower requirement. It has a framework-agnostic WebGPU core and a Svelte 5 adapter. There are no scene objects, cameras, lights, or mesh primitives. The material describes the work, and `<FragCanvas>` manages the renderer that carries it out. Keeping that scope small gives me less API to maintain, clearer failures to explain, and fewer places for bugs to hide.

## Before the first frame

A material is defined once with `defineMaterial()` and treated as immutable. Its fragment entry point must match `fn frag(uv: vec2f) -> vec4f`. Spektral checks that contract at runtime, along with the requirement that uniform and texture keys are valid WGSL identifiers. An attempt to set an unknown uniform throws immediately.

Before compilation, the preprocessor expands `#include` and `#define` directives. It keeps a line map during expansion, so a WGSL compiler error can point to the location in the source I wrote.

The renderer then needs to know whether it can keep using an existing pipeline. I wanted that decision to be easy to inspect. Unexpected recompilation in other WebGPU and WebGL wrappers had made it difficult to tell which changes were expensive.

Spektral derives a deterministic pipeline signature from the compiled shader source, uniform layout, and texture bindings. Live values are excluded. `<FragCanvas>` compares that signature when resolving a material:

| Change | Renderer behavior |
| --- | --- |
| A uniform value changes | Write the dirty range to the GPU buffer; keep the pipeline. |
| `clearColor` changes | Apply it on the next frame; keep the pipeline. |
| Shader source changes | The signature changes, so rebuild the renderer from scratch. |

While the signature stays the same, updates affect uniform buffers and texture bindings without recompilation. The definition tells me where the rebuild boundary is, and that boundary is something I can test.

## Work inside a frame

A frame begins with scheduler tasks. After those run, the renderer resolves uniforms and textures, executes the base fullscreen pass, runs any post-processing passes through the render graph, and presents the result to the canvas.

Each `useFrame` callback is a task with a key, a stage, `before` and `after` dependencies, and an invalidation policy. Stages group tasks and have dependency constraints of their own. Together these form a directed acyclic graph. A topological sort determines the execution order; cycles and missing references throw errors. When an order is unclear, `getSchedule()` exposes the resolved schedule for debugging.

The render mode determines when that work runs:

- `always` keeps the render loop running continuously.
- `on-demand` renders after invalidation.
- `manual` waits for an explicit `advance()` call.

The scheduler is responsible for requesting frames. Changing a value doesn't introduce another implicit path that happens to request a frame. This is particularly useful when I need an effect to stay idle until there is work to do.

Post-processing uses a separate, slot-based render graph. `source` refers to the current scene surface, `target` is its ping-pong companion, and `canvas` is the visible output. Passes can also use named off-screen render targets.

With no passes configured, the base shader renders straight to `canvas`. Adding passes makes `planRenderGraph()` validate their sequence, resolve clear and preserve flags, and produce an immutable execution plan before GPU work starts. A pass that writes to `canvas` in the middle of a chain is rejected. So is reading `target` before anything has written to it, or declaring `needsSwap` on a pass that doesn't run from `source` to `target`.

Once the passes finish, the renderer checks where the result ended up. If it isn't already on `canvas`, it blits the resolved surface there. Three built-in pass types cover common cases, and custom passes use the same interface.

## When rendering fails

A shader compiler message is much easier to act on when the surrounding app can present it consistently. Spektral turns failures such as unavailable WebGPU support, a lost adapter, invalid WGSL, or a uniform type mismatch into a `MotionGPUErrorReport`:

```ts
{
  title: string;       // "WGSL compilation failed"
  message: string;     // Primary error message
  hint: string;        // Suggested fix
  details: string[];   // Additional compiler output
  stack: string[];     // Stack trace
  phase: 'initialization' | 'render';
  source: {            // Present for shader compile errors
    line: number;
    column?: number;
    snippet: Array<{ number: number; code: string; highlight: boolean }>;
  } | null;
}
```

The default error overlay displays the report automatically. It can be disabled with `showErrorOverlay={false}` if I want to handle reports through `onError`, or replaced with `errorRenderer`. Either way, the report has the same shape, including source positions and snippets for shader compilation errors.

Failed renderer creation also has a recovery path. Missing WebGPU support, a WGSL syntax error, or a lost device starts an exponential retry delay: 250 ms, 500 ms, 1000 ms, up to an 8000 ms cap. A change to the pipeline signature resets the backoff. Fixing a shader and hot-reloading it therefore triggers a fresh attempt immediately, rather than waiting for the previous retry delay to expire.

The Svelte adapter is the first way I've exposed this runtime to a framework. React and Vue adapters are on the roadmap; they can use the same core without taking on a dependency on Svelte.
